---
import SabGuide from '../../components/sab/SabGuide.astro';
import { getGuide } from '../../lib/guides';

const guide = getGuide('how-to-write-a-scholarship-essay');
---

<SabGuide guide={guide}>
  <p>
    Scholarship committees read stacks of essays in one sitting. Most of them open the same way: "I have always been passionate about helping others." The reader's eyes slide off it. Your only job is to be the essay that makes them slow down. That is a structure problem, not a talent problem.
  </p>

  <h2>What the reader is actually deciding</h2>
  <p>
    Committees are not grading your writing. They are looking for evidence of the thing their award exists to reward: leadership, resilience, community involvement, academic drive. Reread the award description and underline the nouns. Those nouns are your marking rubric. Compare three real ones: the <a href="/scholarships/keyera-energy-peter-j-renton-memorial-scholarship/">Keyera Energy Peter J. Renton Memorial Scholarship</a> is asking about community contribution, the <a href="/scholarships/fraser-institute-student-essay-contest/">Fraser Institute Student Essay Contest</a> is asking you to argue a position, and the <a href="/scholarships/voice-for-animals-high-school-scholarship/">Voice for Animals High School Scholarship</a> is asking what you actually did. Same essay skills, three different rubrics. An essay about your part-time job can win a leadership award if it shows leadership, and lose a leadership award if it only shows effort.
  </p>

  <h2>A structure that works</h2>
  <h3>1. Start inside a specific moment</h3>
  <p>
    Not your life story, one scene. "The freezer broke at 6 a.m. on the day of our fundraiser bottle drive" beats any sentence containing the word "passionate." Specifics are proof; adjectives are claims.
  </p>
  <h3>2. Show the action you took</h3>
  <p>
    What did you do that someone else in the same spot would not have? This is the middle of the essay and the actual answer to the committee's question. Use "I" and concrete verbs. Numbers help: how many people, how many hours, how much raised.
  </p>
  <h3>3. Say what changed</h3>
  <p>
    In the situation, and in you. One honest sentence about what you got wrong along the way is worth more than a paragraph of triumph. Committees read overconfidence all day; self-awareness stands out.
  </p>
  <h3>4. Connect it to what their money does</h3>
  <p>
    End by linking your plan to the award: what you will study, where it leads, and why the support matters. This is one short paragraph, not a second essay.
  </p>

  <h2>The same paragraph, twice</h2>
  <p>
    The gap between an essay that gets skimmed and one that gets read is almost never vocabulary. It is the distance between telling a committee what kind of person you are and showing them the afternoon that proves it. Here is a real-shaped example of the first kind:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    I have always been passionate about helping my community. Through my volunteer work I developed strong leadership skills and learned the importance of teamwork. This experience taught me that anything is possible with dedication and hard work.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Forty words, and a stranger could have written every one of them about anyone. There is no scene, no action only you took, and nothing a committee could repeat back to another reader. Now the same student, writing the same experience with the abstractions taken out:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    The freezer at the food bank died on a Thursday, and there were three hundred pounds of donated meat in it. I called the two restaurants on Third Street because I knew they had walk-ins, and the second one said yes. We moved it in milk crates in someone's mum's Corolla, six trips. I have never asked a stranger for anything that big before, and I found out that you mostly just have to say the number out loud.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Nearly the same length. But now the committee can see it, and the qualities the first version claimed (initiative, resourcefulness, nerve) are things the reader concluded for themselves rather than things you asserted. That is the entire trick, applied to every paragraph.
  </p>

  <h2>How to re-aim one essay for three awards</h2>
  <p>
    You are not writing a new essay each time. You are re-cutting one piece of raw material against a different rubric, and the edit is usually smaller than students expect: the middle stays, the framing moves.
  </p>
  <p>
    Take the food-bank story. For a community-contribution award like the <a href="/scholarships/keyera-energy-peter-j-renton-memorial-scholarship/">Keyera Peter J. Renton Memorial</a>, the ending is about what the food bank could do afterwards that it could not before. For a leadership award, the ending is about the six trips and who else you got into the car: the decision, not the outcome. For a financial-need bursary, the honest frame is different again: why you were at the food bank on a Thursday afternoon at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    Do the underlining first, every time. Print the award description, underline its nouns, and check that each one appears somewhere in your essay as an event rather than as a word. If "leadership" appears in your essay only because you typed the word "leadership", you have not re-aimed it.
  </p>

  <h2>The mistakes that get essays skipped</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Recycling without re-aiming.</strong> Reusing an essay is smart. Reusing it without adjusting to the award's rubric nouns is the most common way strong students lose.</li>
    <li><strong>Listing instead of showing.</strong> Your activities are already on the application form. The essay is for depth on one or two of them, not breadth on ten.</li>
    <li><strong>Writing to sound impressive.</strong> Thesaurus words and long sentences read as nervous. Write like you talk on a good day.</li>
    <li><strong>Ignoring the word limit.</strong> Hitting 490 of 500 words shows control. Going over shows the opposite, and some portals cut you off mid-sentence.</li>
    <li><strong>Submitting the first draft.</strong> Read it out loud once, then have one adult who knows you read it. Two passes catch almost everything.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to cut when you are over the limit</h2>
  <p>
    Almost every first draft runs 20% long, and the cut is what makes it good. Work in this order and you will rarely have to lose anything that mattered:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>The wind-up.</strong> Your real first sentence is usually the third or fourth one. Delete everything above it.</li>
    <li><strong>Adverbs and intensifiers.</strong> "Really", "very", "incredibly", "truly". They add length and subtract force.</li>
    <li><strong>Anything the application form already says.</strong> Your GPA, your grade, the name of your school.</li>
    <li><strong>The second example.</strong> If you have two stories, the essay is fighting itself. Keep the one with the better scene.</li>
    <li><strong>The moral.</strong> The closing sentence that explains what the story means is almost always the one the committee did not need.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>On using AI to write it</h2>
  <p>
    Worth being blunt, because everyone is asking and few guides will say it: an essay generated wholesale reads like a generated essay. It produces exactly the tone of the first example above (fluent, balanced, entirely unspecific) because that is the average of everything ever written on the topic, and the average is precisely what a committee is trying to filter out. Some awards now ask you to attest that the work is your own, and a reader who spots it does not give you the benefit of the doubt.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where it does help is the parts that are not the writing. Ask it to interview you about an experience so you find the scene. Ask it what a committee for a named award is likely weighing. Paste your own draft in and ask which sentences are claims rather than evidence. All of that leaves the words yours, which is the only thing that makes the essay worth reading.
  </p>

  <h2>How long this actually takes</h2>
  <p>
    A good 500-word essay is about four short sittings, not one long one, and the gaps matter more than the hours:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sitting one (40 minutes).</strong> Write the scene badly and completely. Do not edit while drafting.</li>
    <li><strong>Leave it two days.</strong> This is the step people skip and the one that does the most work.</li>
    <li><strong>Sitting two (30 minutes).</strong> Cut to the limit using the list above.</li>
    <li><strong>Sitting three (15 minutes).</strong> Read it out loud. Every sentence you stumble on gets rewritten.</li>
    <li><strong>Sitting four.</strong> One adult who knows you reads it, then you decide which of their notes to take.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Start a week out, not a night out. Three of those five steps are waiting, and you cannot compress waiting.
  </p>

  <h2>If you think you have nothing to write about</h2>
  <p>
    You do not need a dramatic story. Committees in Alberta read winning essays about coaching a younger sibling's hockey team, translating at a parent's appointments, and keeping a job through exam season. Ordinary responsibilities, described specifically and honestly, are the material. What sinks essays is vagueness, not a quiet life.
  </p>
  <p>
    Once the essay exists, it compounds: most awards ask similar questions, so your second application takes a quarter of the time. Build your list with <a href="/match/">the match quiz</a> and put the essay to work more than once; the <a href="/scholarships/mensa-canada-scholarship-programme/">Mensa Canada Scholarship Programme</a> and <a href="/scholarships/canadas-luckiest-student/">Canada's Luckiest Student</a> both run on one, and neither asks for marks.
  </p>
</SabGuide>
